SC: No. I still remembered them. I remembered my parents. But when Miriam asked me: Do you remember your parents? And I remember that moment that she asked me. I turned my head around. And I didn't say anything. I didn't want to lie to her and say no. But I couldn't tell anybody the truth because my parents, over and over again had said: Don't ever ask for us. Don't ever. If anybody mention us, you don't know what they're talking about. Now, how do you tell a five-year-old who is in a destroyed city where walls are falling down and ceilings are are nonexistent of big, enormous buildings in my eyes, where there are tanks running up and down the streets, where there are soldiers with machine guns. How do you tell a child the war is over? I had no inkling that war was over, so I wasn't going to talk about my parents. So I kept mute, not answering any questions until I set one foot in Israel.

INT: Let's take a break for a couple minutes before we move on to Israel.

SC: Okay.

INT: So we'll cut and take a quick break.

SC: All right. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS]

INT: Okay, we're about to go to Israel. Is there anything else you want to mention before we leave Europe?

SC: There is. The very beginning, when the Nazis came into Kovno, it happened... The actual attack happened on a Sunday morning. I mean, in the early hours of Sunday. And in June. And it was the Nazis had broken their pact, the non-aggression pact with Russia. And they had attacked Russia by coming into Lithuania. So Monday came and my nanny did not show up and we were running out of milk for me. So by Tuesday it was decided that my parents and my grandmother and my cousin, we would all go to my grandfather's farm, which was about a few miles out of the city, and in the outskirts. And that there would be... It would be safer because the Lithuanian people, the non-Jews, were breaking down Jewish stores and attacking Jewish people and killing Jewish people. And there was a very big anti-Semitism in Lithuania. And so we would be safer at my grandfather's farm, my grandfather boasted with his good relationship with his non-Jewish neighbor farmers. So it was decided that we would all walk through the city in the back streets, and that my mother would be the one to carry me, because she was the one who looked less Jewish, that she would walk a few steps ahead of them, and if they were stopped, she would keep on walking with me toward safety. And we got to my grandfather's house, safe and sound, and everybody was very happy to see us. A lot of the family members were there, and also the neighbors and two of the neighbors and their son. And after a while after eating and relaxing, my father said to my mother: I don't like the atmosphere in this house. I don't trust these people. The neighbors. When I speak to them, they don't look... They do not look me in the face. They avert their eyes. I want to leave now. My mother said to him: You're crazy. You're very nervous about the situation. Rightly so, but these people are friends. They... You know. My father said: You want to stay, you stay. I'm taking my family and the baby out of here. So, of course, my mother went with him and they went back to the city. And it was maybe an hour or so after we had left that the so-called neighbor showed up, the friend, with the militia, the Lithuanian partisans, and they took my grandfather, his two sons, and his son-in-law, and they took them to one of the fortresses, the seventh fort, where they killed everybody they took in in the next few days. And that's how the nightmare of the Holocaust started for my family.

INT: And do you know what year that was?

SC: Excuse me?

INT: The year.

SC: The year? Yes. That was 1941. June of 1941. The Nazis had not come in yet to the city. [PAUSE 6 SECONDS]

INT: Before the break we were about to start your voyage to Palestine, to Israel.

SC: Yes.

INT: Can you tell us about that? That trip? You got as far as saying you boarded a ship? What happened?

SC: Well, I got on board of the ship and we were sleeping on the floor, and it was very scary for me to be on the ship because I had never seen the ocean. And it, in my eyes, the ship was enormous. And there there were people all over the place, and it probably was choppy. I don't remember that very much, but we finally, finally got to Palestine. And it was... It was close to Shabbat. It was...The Sabbath was coming. So we stayed on the ship, Miriam and us, and we left, I guess, on Sunday. We... We came to to to Haifa and I found out later because I had visions of it and I figured maybe I'm imagining it, but I had visions of how I disembarked that ship. And it was they didn't have the boats on Sunday. They weren't going to lower down boats for a few people. So I don't know how Miriam got on this small boat, but I was hoisted down with a rope. I remember her arms outstretched to grab me. Took me 30 years to go on a cruise because it was so frightening, the idea of getting on and off a boat. But anyways, I remember coming into Haifa and I remember getting on the pier and all these people who had come to see the miracle of, you know, children who had been saved from Hitler. And, you know, all the time that we were traveling in Europe and it was so tough on us, we children, we kept saying with the promise that we were going to the land of milk and honey. We were going to have everything. Once we got there, we were not going to have to be afraid anymore. We were going to have chocolate, whatever that was. We never tasted a piece. We were going to be free and safe. So I figured in my mind, land of milk and honey, it's going to deliver my parents. They're going to be there. The Promised Land. So not being able to discuss this with anybody, so as not to give myself and my parents away, I kept it well inside of me, but the minute I put one foot on that port, on that pier, you know, I looked. I looked to find my parents and there were nowheres around. So I remember a man was standing next to me and I turned around and I said: Where's my mother? Where's my father? And the truth would have been, I don't know and nobody else in this world does. But they lied to me and they said, next port. Next thing, next village, next city, next place. And finally, after riding in a jeep from one place to the other, from... There were no modern roads at that time, so it took a long time to get from Haifa to Jerusalem. And I burst into tears. I just started screaming: I want my mother, I want my mother! And so I still remember somebody putting a piece of banana in my mouth. You see, in Lithuania, in Europe, bananas were like, what do I know, the most... I mean, caviar was, I think, less expensive than a banana in that part of the world. And so it took me 30 years to be able to eat a banana, because just the smell of it made me gag from that moment. And the next thing I knew, we were in a jeep and then in another car, and it was already nightfall. And I don't remember this, but my foster sister does, and she remembers it ,that I said: Stop lying to me. I know I'm an orphan. And that very same night I started calling Miriam which is Hebrew for mother, and I was sure my parents were gone. They're never coming back. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS] I remember that moment when they took me out of the of the car. Somebody picked me up and ran me to the house and took me to an old lady who was in bed, and it was Miriam's mother. And she started: Gittele, Gittele, and I said: I'm not Gittele and I'm not your granddaughter. And I was very upset. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS]

INT: Do you remember what people said to you to try to comfort you with this time?

SC: I don't think anybody really tried to comfort me. [PAUSES] I think, you know, we had gone through so much. Everybody has gone through so very much. I don't think people were aware of what... of children's feelings. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS]

INT: Where did you ultimately go when you stopped going from jeep to car to... Where were you settling?

SC: Well, we settled in Jerusalem in what was then the Yeshiva too. That's where they came to learn. And there was Miriam's father and mother lived there, and it was a small house, so we were overcrowded. And I never knew which bed I was going to sleep in, which room. But I felt that I was, you know, I wasn't afraid, although there was still shooting at night. There was... I remember going under the bed many, many times and praying with the other, with my foster sister who was only two years older than me. And because there was a lot of unrest in Palestine at that time between the British and the Arabs and us. [PAUSES] And I remember we made a pilgrimage. We walked from the new city to the old city, to the citadel. And I went to the wall. I saw the wall in 1945, '46. This was already '46 we went. And I remember I was just jumping around and, you know, I was a kid. I was five and a half. And I remember one of the rabbis there turning me around to face the wall and said: Stop carrying on. Better cry for your parents and the 6 million Jews. [PAUSES]

INT: So does that mean by this point you already knew about the six million or...

SC: I guess at this point I knew that all the Jews had been killed, all the Jews, and that I considered my parents gone. [PAUSES]

INT: While all this was happening with you, that your travels through Europe and then into Palestine, what was happening with your parents?

SC: Well, my mother was liberated on... in... at the end of January of '45. She had been in Stutthof concentration camp, which was near Danzig in Poland, and had been on the death march. They had been taken to a subcamp in that area. And she survived the Death March. And they had been placed in that subcamp. And then on January 31st, there was no roll call, no screaming, no carrying on from the Nazi guards. So little by little, the, you know, the women started coming out of the barracks and then they saw the Russian army coming in and they were liberated. But the Russians had hardly any... anything themselves. I mean, my mother said that when she saw who the Russian army was, she was surprised they had won because she said that there were so... They were almost kids that had been taken into the army that the rifles were were taller than they were. And they, you know, they had all these women that they had to take... that they were in charge of. They didn't just set them free. They took them to a camp in a place called [NON-ENGLISH] in... near, Danzig, that was like a... They had those special spa waters, a mineral waters, springs, mineral springs. And so they were there. I mean, they did not harm them, but they were still not free. They were still now under the Russian regime, and my mother was very sick from malnutrition. She had frostbite from walking in the snow with hardly any... I mean, you couldn't call them shoes. And she was in the Russian hospital there. And then when she came out of the hospital, a young man came to the camp looking for his girlfriend. See, the Nazis were so methodical that you could kind of follow their route. So all the women that were left in the Kovno ghetto, they had all been taken to Stutthof, and he was looking for his girlfriend. So he figured if she survived, this is where she would be. So he came there and the women were milling around. Where do you come from? I come from Kovno. Who's left? He was in hiding. Who was left in hiding with you? And he starts enumerating people and he mentions... And there is this the Rebbetzin Shulman, Miriam Shulman. She saved her children and other children, and she's now on her way to Palestine with her children. And do you remember the redheaded guy who was in charge of the bread distribution in the ghetto? She's got his child, too. And that's... And my mother fainted. That was the first news that my mother had that I had survived. So my mom started making her plans. And a few days later... Oh, he told her that he was going back to Kovno to see if he could find his girlfriend. Maybe she was there and he hadn't found her. And his aunt was a doctor in the hospital where my mother had been, the Russian hospital. So he said, I'll verify about your daughter. And I will send a telegram to my aunt to let you know. But I know that their plans were that they were going to ÅÃ³dÅº, the city of ÅÃ³dÅº, to first, and that's where they would be. So he sent her. He sent that telegram with two words in Polish which means "daughter lives." So my mother made her plans and she escaped the camp the very next day right in front of the guards with the machine guns. She told them that she was going to the well to get water, and she made a run for it. She hid in somebody's garden, a little house in the village. And when, you know darkness fell, she knocked on the door and begged them and begged them. And finally they took her in and they were very nice to her. And a few days later, when they felt they weren't looking for her anymore, they went [UNCLEAR] took her to the village, and there she got on a broken down bus and train and for one thing or the other, no money, no documents, sick whatever, my mother made the trek through Europe and finally got to ÅÃ³dÅº. And when she got to ÅÃ³dÅº, she went to the joint distribution, which kept tabs on people, on the people who had survived. And she found that they told her we were already on the boat. We had already sailed for Palestine. My mother came out of there and she was crying because all she had was her torn shoes, the dress, no underwear. No money. Sick and he could not swim the ocean. She figured she'll never, ever see me again. She had not inquired about my father because when she got liberated and she was told who had died the first day, the first week in the camp, the second week, they were all people who were, you know, laborers or young and strong. And my father was... had a nervous stomach, was a chain smoker, and hadn't done any physical work in his life. So she figured he's gone. I mean, and he wasn't on the list, but she was standing in the in a street in ÅÃ³dÅº when these two young men, still boys, recognized her, passed her and recognized her and said: Aren't you Riva Baicovitz? Your husband is alive. He's in Germany. He's in Munich, working for the Americans. And he knows you are alive because there are lists and all that. We're going there. We couldn't find our parents anywhere. We're going back. Come with us. And that's how my mother started her trek to reunite with my father. Who knew that she would be smart enough to come to him and not have him come to the Russian part of Europe, which was a one way street. And my mother and these two boys walked, took broken down trains, broken down busses, broken down trucks, whatever method they could find. They stole bicycles, they rolled down hills, and they got to the border that separated the area, Munich, the Russian sector and the American sector. And we had a deal with the Russians. We were still friends with them then before the Cold War, that if you didn't have documents that showed that you lived in the Munich area before the war, you were sent back to the Russian sector. So of course, these three people had nothing that was even close to documents. And so they were taken off the train and put on a train going back to the Russian sector, and these two guys and my mother, they climbed to the roof of the train, and when the train they had been taken off past them, they jumped from one moving train to the other. And that's how my mom got reunited with my dad. [PAUSES] My dad had survived by a hair, Dachau concentration camp. They... He was not liberated by Eisenhower troops. He was already out of the camp. They had taken a whole bunch of inmates and put them on a train, probably to shoot them someplace else or leave them to die someplace else. He was already... My father was 5'10" and he weighed 80 pounds at Liberation. So... And they figured, you know, take a whole bunch of them and just get rid of the corpses somehow. And the train my father was on was being bombed by the Americans. So the guards didn't want to die. So they said: So it was raining, it was April and it was really miserable but then the bombing stopped. So the guard said, and I still remember my father telling me the story, the guards came and said: And my father said the word He hadn't heard that from a German in years. Things must be very bad for them when they're saying And so he didn't come back to the train. And so others stayed in the forest. And they could see the lights of the village on the other side of the forest, but they did not know whether the Americans were already in that particular village, had taken it over, or they were still in German hands. So they stayed until hunger and every... You know, and the wetness, the dampness took over and they... One man came running into the forest saying that... Yelling: You're free! Jews, you're free! Come to the village! And my father didn't know if he was hallucinating or not. Or just, you know, from the hunger and everything else. And the man had a big black bread that he was carrying, and my father tested him to see if he was telling the truth or not. And he said: Okay, if the Americans are still there, there's plenty of bread. Will you give me the bread? He said: Sure. And my father said if he was giving up his bread, it was true. And so he walked into the village and that's how he got liberated.

And he spoke English. So he started working for the Americans and the Displaced person camp in the outskirts of Munich. And he had found out also by word of mouth and by list, that my mother was alive, and that I was alive, that Miriam had saved a bunch of children, and we were all on the way to Palestine. My father's brothers, all of them, with the exception of one brother and my father, had left for Cuba when my father was like 14 years old, 16. And so he had made himself remember one address of one of his brothers, and he had sent letters through the Red Cross telling them that I existed. That, you know, we were alive, that I was on my way to Palestine or was in Palestine, to please find me, to make sure I was okay. And, you know, the letters just... It was right after the war. It was... The mail was almost impossible from Europe. And somehow somebody took a letter to Paris and mailed it from Paris. And it came to Cuba. That one arrived. But the brother, he was writing, the one address he had remembered, that brother had died suddenly of a heart attack, and his family had moved from that address. But the Cubans are very nice people. And the mailman kept thinking: Look at the stamps. They are so unusual. It's... I'm not going to just take it back. There is a Jewish person who has a store in the area. Maybe he knows the family. I'm going to go take it to them. So he did. And this man did know my uncle, my other uncle. And he called him and he said: Okay. So, the widow had relatives. My uncle's widow had relatives in Paris. Maybe it's from them. He said: Just open the letter, tell me who signs it. And the man did, and he said, your brother Zalman. And that's how my family in Cuba found out we were alive and that I existed.

And my uncle immediately started doing, you know, papers for my parents and contacted family that we had in Israel. Cousins. And he made papers so that my parents could leave Germany. And they went to Paris, and he got them in Paris, brought them to Cuba. Because everything was, you know, documentation. In Europe it was impossible almost if he hadn't had some pull. In Cuba, it would have been impossible and nobody was going to getting papers for Palestine. It was so difficult because it was still a British mandate. And so he brought them to Cuba. My father was too sick to travel. But the family accompanied my mother to New York, and there she found the one method of getting to Palestine was on a mercantile marine ship. And that's how they got reunited. And then came the part that when I was told that my parents were alive. And that I will, like, it happened today. I remember Miriam called me over and she was sitting in the dining room and she said: I got this in the mail. And: See it's a picture. And: Tell me if you know who these people are. And I immediately... I said: I don't know who this man is. It was my uncle or one of my uncles, my mother's brother. I said: I remember how my heart skipped a beat and I pointed and I said: This is my mother. This was my mother. And if the man In the picture had red hair, that was my father. And Miriam embraced me and she said: They still are. They are your parents. They're alive and they're coming to get you. And I didn't believe her. I did not believe her. Been lied to before. But then a package came and there was a ring for me and a ring for my foster sister, Abigail, and a little toy. And... I still... It was hard to believe. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS]

INT: Before we go much further I want to ask you, when did your relatives move to Cuba?

SC: My relatives started moving to Cuba...

My father, one of my father's eldest brother moved there first. And then I had one other brother moved there. Then there was the sister. My father's sister moved there. Then another brother who had lost a child in Lithuania to scarlet fever, needed a change of place, you know. And he left on the last boat that went out of Italy before the war, and he came to Cuba. So the only ones that were left was my father in Kovno and a brother of his in Tavrig, Lithuania. It's a town. It was about maybe 100 miles from... couple hundred miles from Kovno. He was left with his family, with his two daughters and wife. Their son, my cousin But you know what happened in those towns, they killed everybody. So that family didn't make it. [PAUSES] But then my parents and I were reunited, the three of us in Cuba in February of 19... My mother came to Israel. We had to wait because all of a sudden Cuba decided that they were not giving me the documentation I needed to get into Cuba. They didn't want any more people in Cuba. Jews, whatever. But my uncle's lawyer, his wife, was very friendly with a senator. And she went out... a senator's wife... and she went out with her and showed her my picture and somehow the papers materialized, and on February 6th of 1947, I was reunited with my father in Cuba. [PAUSES] And it was a miracle. The three of us. 'Cause Lithuania in Kovno... I mean, only like 2000 people out of the 38,000 or 40,000 survived, and only about 150 children under the age of 12 survived. So it was a total miracle that the three of us, a family left intact. [PAUSES]

INT: Can you tell us about the moment that you were reunited with your mother and your father and what that moment was like for you?

SC: Oh, boy. Well, what happened was that when my mother arrived in Israel I was not living at Miriam's. I had spent time with relatives. I had relatives from my father's side in Tel Aviv, and I had been there, but they brought me back to Jerusalem because rightly so, Miriam said when her mother picks her up, it will be from me. And it was the right thing. But Miriam's mother had had another stroke. The house was very small and they couldn't have little children running around, so they sent my foster sister Abigail to some relatives, and I was at somebody's, friend of the family, I don't even remember her name, living in her place. At the time my mother came and it was in the old City of Jerusalem, the Citadel. And I had been told that my mother was coming, but I didn't believe it. And part of me was, you know, I want her to come. and part of me was ambivalent about even trusting whatever I was being told. So I remember very, very clearly that I was getting a bath in a tub when my oldest foster sister and foster brother came, and they whispered something into the woman ear and I heard the word "mother." I said: Is it about my mother? And they said: No, no, no, no, no, Why? I haven't done anything. When [HEBREW] spoke to you. It was to reprimand you. Surely because you had not... You hadn't behaved the way you should. I've been good. I haven't done a thing. Said: No, no. Come on, come on. And we walked. I guess we didn't have that much money for busses and stuff. And we walked to the other side to the new city. And I still remember we went in to buy me some sandals and there were white sandals and I said: [HEBREW] is not going to be happy about the shoes you're buying me, because these are not going to last. And we didn't have money, so they said: No, it's okay, it's okay. But why does [HEBREW] want to see me? I don't know, she'll tell you. And I came running around the bed screaming, and I haven't done anything bad. And I came around the bend of the house, and then this lady in a blue dress came out. And she stopped in her track. My mother was coming out to see where was I? Why wasn't I being brought to her? And I stopped in mine. I mean, it was a shock. And then she opened her arms and I flew into them. I recognized her immediately. Then I froze. [PAUSE 6 SECONDS] And then came the tough part, because I first I didn't have a mother, now I had two. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS] Took time, took time to... You know, it was difficult. It was very difficult for both women. And I remember my mother would say to me: Come on, let's go and I'll buy you an ice cream cone. And I loved ice cream. And I would turn around to Miriam and say, real mother to go to get ice cream?

So, you know, it was a tough situation. And I think the two women were jealous of each other. And as a matter of fact, they converged many years later in New York when I was I was living in New York, the first summer I was there, and Miriam came to visit her daughter, Abigail, who was living in New York, and my mother came to visit me. And I remember there was friction there. I mean, my mother was so ever grateful to Miriam, but I... You know, I could tell. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS] It was...

And then I came to Cuba and I became a princess. I had all this family, all these cousins whom I adored, who adored me, my aunts and uncles. And it was a charmed childhood from there on.

INT: Can you share with us your reunion with your father?

SC: Yes. My father was very, very skinny then. And I still remember when we finally got to Havana. I mean, it was from New York to Miami, layover for hours in Miami. And then finally the the plane landed in Havana, and we were in customs and sitting, waiting for them to call our name to get out of customs, and it was... There was a wall separating, you know, customs from the rest of the airport. And it wasn't... The wall did not touch the ceiling. There was an opening. And my father was trying to just show his face. And I said: There's your daddy, there's your daddy. And I came to... I came to... We came out of customs. And then I remember he, you know, he he went like, on his knees to embrace me and... I remember how his body was shaking with his sobs. [CRYING] He's sobbing. He was sobbing so. He was all bones. And I cried too. And then somebody later said: You were crying because you were happy to see your daddy? I said: He was crying so I cried too. When grown-ups cry, you cry, when they laugh, you laugh. That's how wise I was. And then we... I remember the whole family had come and my uncle brought a shiny car, I had never seen anything so beautiful in my life. And I know it was a Buick and it was blue and it was gorgeous. And, you know, in those times the cars were very big. You could pile up a lot of people. I was in the back seat sitting with my... on my father's lap. And then I asked a question and everybody laughed and I couldn't understand why they were laughing. I thought my question was very to the point, and the question was I wanted to know if the taxi was my uncle's or he leased it from somebody. I had never met anybody who owned a car. [PAUSES] I thought I... Very smart, very normal question. And, you know, I bonded with my father a lot quicker than I did with my mother.

He had gone through a lot, but somehow he was able to put my feelings on top of his list, and he was much more understanding of... I had some temper tantrums.

You know, it was very willful at times. My mother. .. Between the two of them, between my father and my mother, they lost 22 members of their family. [PAUSE 6 SECONDS] And I had to start a new life in a new place with a terrible climate.

You know, coming from a place like Lithuania to a place... to the tropics.

And air conditioning was not the norm in those days. But it was a good life and it was a good extended family. And we stayed there until Castro came and then my parents sent me out again. I was one of the first ones to leave Cuba for my family. And... my parents felt that if Castro would stop people from leaving Cuba, the first ones would be still students. People young enough to brainwash and did not want me to go through being a semi-prisoner again. So when Castro declared himself a communist and I was told I had four days to pack and get out and I came to Miami.

INT: When was this?

SC: 1960. July of 1960.

INT: Before we have you leave Cuba is there anything else you would like to share with us about your time there? and could you put it into a time frame? When did you arrive? You just told us when you left.

SC: I arrived in Cuba on February 6th, 1947. And I left on July 13th, 1960. [PAUSES]

INT: Did you witness any anti-Semitism in Cuba?

SC: Not really. Not really. I... The only... A little girl told me when I was still, I mean, maybe I had been in Cuba a couple of years at the most, said her grandmother didn't want me to... want her to play with me because I was Jewish. And I said: Why? I thought... You know, when I came to Cuba, and I tell this a lot, especially when I speak to children, my world was so divided. It was us Jewish people and the enemy. That was it, because even in Israel, the Arabs were the enemy, the British were the enemy, you know. So I was... I come there and we moved to this new... It was a new area and into an apartment. And all the neighbors were crazy about me and loved me and brought me little gifts and treats. And my parents got along with everybody. And so I figured here I was. My world is Christians. I mean Jewish people and the enemy. So all these people are so good to me. The whole of Cuba is Jewish as far as I'm concerned. Until Christmas came, of course. And then, you know, I couldn't have a Christmas tree and that was a whole to-do. But that's when I realized, you know, and that there is goodness in everybody. But that little girl told me that her grandmother wouldn't let me play... wouldn't let her play with me. Because why? Why Jews? Because Jews killed Jesus Christ. And I said: Who was he? I had no idea. So she... I said: It's impossible. Jews don't kill anybody. Jews get killed. They don't kill. So she took took me to her grandmother, who read me from a book. It was in print. And to me, anything that was in print was 100%, because the only thing, I still didn't know how to read and write, so the only thing I had ever seen was the Bible, you know, and special books for children and stuff like that, all religious. So I said: Okay. You know, I was really very upset. So I came and I told my father and he said: Okay, so what did you say? And I told him, I said: Well, Daddy, I had to admit it because it was in print. I mean, it was in a book. They say it was in their Bible. And so I told them: Okay. So all right, the Jews killed, but it wasn't the Lithuanian Jews. And that's how I got out of it. So that was my very first experience. My second experience was when I was like about 14. I had a teacher in school who didn't like me. I went to a very progressive school, an American school, and I didn't know why, she just didn't like me. It was... I got along with everybody. I was a pretty normal kid. And one day, and she always had this test, this quick one question test on Fridays, and then she would discuss it on Monday, and then Monday she came and she said: It was terrible what you kids did last Friday. Nobody has really paid any attention. And you, and she turned to me, you wrote equator with a... in Spanish, with a Q instead of a C. I said: Oh, I'm so sorry. We called our teachers, they had a, you know, a doctorate degree... ...it's the influence of English. I was taking Spanish high school and English high school simultaneously. She says: I don't care if it was English influence or Jewish influence. I said: Oh, now I know why she doesn't like me. And I went to my father and I said: Look, daddy, what happened? Are you going to go talk to the principal? He said: Nope. I said: What do you mean? He says: You're going to go and talk to the teacher or the principal. You're going to encounter this problem throughout your life, and you're going to have to deal with it. Are you either going to be up front about it or not. But this is the only thing I can give you is to make you ready for whatever comes. And I still remember how dry my mouth was when I stayed after class, because I was basically a little shy, and to talk to this woman and to confront her. And I said to her that I never knew why she didn't like me, but that I had found out a few days before and that it was that she was anti-Semitic, but that I was going to stay in her class. And she was... And it was her problem and not mine. And she kept saying: Oh no, no, no, please don't. I said: I'm not going to the principal. This is your problem and not mine. And she was I mean, I became her pet. I had her again two years later, my junior year, and I could do no wrong. But that is the only time I ever felt anything in Cuba. [PAUSE 6 SECONDS]

INT: What did being Jewish mean to you at this point, given all your experiences in Europe and then in Palestine and Cuba? What did being Jewish mean for you?

SC: It was being different from the majority of people. But being Jewish has always seemed something to be very proud of because I think we're a very strong people. We are a people who have survived a lot of hatred. And I think in general we don't carry that hatred in us and we try to do good, and we have given the world the best of us. There are plenty of Jewish people who are nasty SOBs, I'm sure. But I find I like most of the people that I have met. I love what my parents were. And a lot of it had to do with their belief in the goodness of man. My parents were true survivors. They got their bodies, but they never got their soul. [PAUSE 9 SECONDS]

INT: So you left Cuba and went to, you said, Miami. What were your expectations of the United States and what were your first impressions of the United States?

SC: I had been in the United States as a tourist before. And, as a matter of fact, I had been in for about two months in New York, the university in Cuba was closed at that time so I stayed with relatives in New York. And it was a fun place, a place where you could go shopping. There was so much shopping in America. And I spoke fluent English because my father sent me, my mother sent me to an American school.

And when I came to Miami, it was supposedly for a very short time because Cuba would open up. We've expected America would never allow communism to be to come 90 miles off the coast of the United States. So I came middle of July. My parents came to see me in September. And I had gotten a job.

And they left, back, end of September. And on October, I think it was 20th, the... all the major businesses in Cuba were confiscated by Castro's regime. And my father came back to Miami a day later. My mother stayed packing up stuff and came a couple of weeks later. And then then when, you know, the realization that I couldn't go back, that there was no home. That's when it hit me. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS]

INT: Where did your family settle in the United States?

SC: Well, my parents came to Charlotte. I stayed in Miami, and then I came to Charlotte. And Charlotte was very... It was 1962, end of '62... was a small... It was still a small town. And... I... My uncle, the one who brought us to Cuba, got killed in a car accident in November of '62. And that was a terrible blow for the whole family. And I stayed here until February of the following year and my father kicked me out, Said: This is not a place for you. And there was no money for me to go to school. You couldn't go to City College or anything like that because you had to be a resident. And I came as a first as a tourist and then as a refugee. And because I was born in Lithuania, the quota was closed. So I couldn't apply. So I was really stateless. And so I couldn't go to college, so I left. My father really said: You got to go to New York. And so I went to New York and I got a job using my Spanish in an export company. And I remember I called my dad, sprung for the money to call, to say I got a job, a very high position. And he said: Really? How much are they paying you? And I said: $70 a week. He said: What's so great about that? You were making almost that money in Miami. And I said: I'm on the 75th floor of the Empire State Building. I can't go much higher than that. And I stayed there for 21 years. And I became a... I ended up continuing the business of my own in export until I came here to get involved in my dad's business with my husband. [PAUSES]

INT: When you came to the United States, how many languages did you speak?

SC: Well, only Yiddish, my mother tongue. I had forgotten my Hebrew. And Spanish and English. [PAUSE 9 SECONDS]

INT: Did anybody in Cuba or in the United States ask you about your early years in the ghetto and what you went through during the war?

SC: Nobody in Cuba knew about it of my friends. The ones that asked me was my family, my aunts, my uncles. I would tell the story when I was a child, but then I stopped talking about it altogether. I guess I didn't want to be different. And never spoke about it here in the United States. And the... I started speaking publicly in schools when I was 50 years old. My father passed away suddenly and his banking... banker came to the office to give me his condolences. And he said: You know, your father had promised my son that he would come to his seventh grade and tell his story. Will you do it for him? And how could I say no? So I went and when I saw the reaction of the kids and the letters that I got afterwards, I realized that I must continue doing so. [PAUSES]

INT: You mentioned your husband. What did you tell your family? Your husband, children, grandchildren. What did you tell them about your early years?

SC: Well, I have no... I had no children. I married late in life. I was 40 years old and my husband was a lot older than I was. He fooled me. Didn't tell me the truth about his age. And I didn't have to tell my husband much because my husband was in World War II. He liberated Nordhausen. His battalion liberated Nordhausen concentration camp in Austria. So he knew. He didn't talk about it. But he... [PAUSES]

INT: You mentioned earlier flashbacks.

SC: Mm-hmm.

INT: I'm going to ask you now, have you experienced flashbacks or nightmares about your childhood?

SC: I have had no nightmares, but I have had flashbacks and I have had times when, you know, it comes. It's... It's difficult to explain, but when we first moved here, Richard, my husband, he would still have his business in New York, and he was not... He loved to drive. And he would drive back and forth to New York, and he would leave, usually on a Sunday morning, very, very early, almost at dawn, and... to avoid traffic. And that day, I would be very, very down. And I'm usually not a person who gets depressed, but I would have a very depressing day. And at the beginning, my father would say to me: Are you worried? You know, because of my uncle in a car accident. He'll be fine. He'll be fine. I'd say: No. It's just funny, dark, heavy mood. And then one day it just came out without my even realizing it. He was leaving the next day, and I said: Would you please? Again and again, I've asked you to wake me before you leave. He said: I'm leaving at four in the morning. It's Sunday, you can sleep. Please wake me. You don't understand. I said when I wake up, the way the light comes in here, in this bedroom, it's like in the basement when I woke up all alone. And it came out and that was the mood the whole day.

So I guess it's in the subconscious, very deeply buried there. [PAUSE 5 SECONDS]

INT: Have you ever been back to Europe, to some of the places where you were before?

SC: No, never. I have never gone back to Lithuania. My dad made me promise. He said: don't go there. It's covered with our blood. And, you know, the Lithuanians killed. They were... I just found out there were only a thousand Nazis that came to Lithuania. And there were 95% of the Jewish population was killed in Lithuania, and a lot of it was killed at the hands of the Lithuanians. So there's nothing to go back to. I mean, I the ghetto was burned and dynamited. I was eight months old when I left the city.

I don't even know the address of where we lived. And, no. And I haven't been back to Cuba, either.

Go forward. I go forward.

INT: What is it like for you to be an American?

SC: I think it's wonderful. I think you have to be coming from a different place to really, really realize what a wonderful country this has been.

For me anyways. It's a country of opportunity. It's a country where you can express yourself. So many people express themselves a little too much in many bad ways with guns. But it's a wonderful, wonderful country. And I'm very grateful. [PAUSE 6 SECONDS]

INT: What are your thoughts about the situation in Israel since October 7th?

SC: It's very, very horrible. What happened was just terrible. I pray. I go to sleep with a prayer for everybody in Israel and I wake up with that same thought. I am very upset that it happened. I'm upset that people who are... who are innocent are getting killed. And I'm more than upset that there has been such horrible reactions from a lot of population and university population and young population against Israel, against Jewish people in general, and that everybody can be at war, but when Israel is at war, we're all of a sudden killers. I mean, this is war. This... What happened, in my opinion, was Israel's Pearl Harbor. And the United States, we defend it, and we defend it, that attack.

And I pray for it to be over. [PAUSE 6 SECONDS]

INT: Before we conclude, what have we not talked about that you would like to add?

SC: Well, the girls that are from the Butterfly... that Butterfly project that we do at the JCC, it's a wonderful program that I am very involved with in which we bring in children from schools and tell them with lessons about the Holocaust, and they listen to a survivor tell their story and have the opportunity to answer or to ask questions and get answers from somebody who is real and went through it. And they mentioned to me that when, you know, I tell them that I have one photograph of me at the age of three... You know, all the photographs were burned with the ghetto and everything else. I have lots of photographs because of before, of my family, before, because one of my aunts left Lithuania, my mother's sister, and went to South Africa, and so her siblings kept sending photos. So I have... And then my grandmother had a sister, my paternal grandmother had a sister in Yonkers, New York, so she got some photographs. So I have those. but I have this one photograph of me with my very first haircut. Okay? And I was very upset. It was my very first bad hair day. And it's... it was taken so that my father could take this... It was taken in the ghetto, and, you know, you had to be very careful because you were not allowed to have cameras. So this one person had a camera and took the photo. And this is the photo my father took from place to place of people when he interviewed people who would be possible saviors.

The reason I have this photograph is because when my father got to Dachau, they were easing off on some of their restrictions there because they were already losing the war. Father got there in July of 1944. So they were losing the war, and they were also building a underground hangar for planes in Dachau. And they needed the slave labor. So they allowed the inmates that were coming in to keep two items. So most of the men were keeping their watch and whatever. My father gave up his watch to keep my photograph because he felt that the watch might buy him some extra rations, but that would go, and the photograph would allow him to hang on and live another day. So that's how I get the photograph. But it's... I am not a happy camper on that photograph. And my father always said it because I was scared of the camera. I had never seen one. That was not it. I had just been taken to a barber and they had cut my locks. I had beautiful long hair and I was a very unhappy camper. [LAUGHING] Still remember the day.

INT: I have one more question for you. What message do you have for future viewers of this interview? [PAUSES] Don't... Don't let the... My message is that don't let hate ever, ever get into your heart. [PAUSES] Just consider that we are all the same. We can pray to a different God. We can have different color eyes, different color skin. But good people are good people. And just take that word and just burn it someplace far away from you. Because it destroys you. It doesn't destroy the object of your dislike. [PAUSES] Be good to one another. That's all I can say.

INT: Thank you. Thank you for speaking with us today.

SC: Thank you. Thank you to Mr. Spielberg. [PAUSE 9 SECONDS]

