SC: It was taken in Kovno, Lithuania, 1935, '36.

INT: Thank you.

And who is this, please?

SC: This is my father, Zalman Baicovitz. Probably at the age also of 20, 21.

INT: And do you know when or where the photo was taken?

SC: The photo was taken in Kovno, Lithuania.

I would say he must have been about 21 years old, so it would have been 1932, '33.

INT: Thank you. And who is this?

SC: This is my grandmother, my paternal grandmother, [?Sarah Minda?] Baicovitz. And she's the one who made the prophecy that because I was born on the first day of the Jewish New Year on Rosh Hashanah, I would be lucky all my life.

INT: And do you know when or where the photo was taken?

SC: I don't. I don't know where it was taken or if it was in Kovno or in another city, nor do I know her age at that time.

INT: Okay. Thank you. And who are these people?

SC: The younger child is me. And the girl standing next to me is who was in the same house in the ghetto that we were in. And this was taken on my third birthday, which would have been October 3rd, 1943, in the ghetto, the Kovno ghetto in Lithuania.

INT: And your full name, please.

SC: Shulamit Baicovitz Chenkin.

INT: Thank you. What are we looking at here?

SC: This is our basically traveling document permitting us to go to Palestine. This is Miriam, myself, Shulamit, and my two foster sisters, Esther and Abigail.

INT: And can you give everybody's last name, please?

SC: It's Miriam Schulman. Abigail and Esther Schulman. [PAUSES]

INT: And your last name?

SC: Baicovitz. [PAUSES]

INT: Thank you. Can you please tell us about this picture?

SC: This is the picture that was shown to me by Miriam Schulman and I was asked: Do you know who these people are? And picture that I recognize my mother and father.

INT: And your parents names, please? Can you tell?

SC: Riva and Zalman Baicovitz.

